% ============================================================================
% mitosis-phase-ordinal-position — each mitotic phase's position in the cycle
% as an ORDINAL WORD ("third", not just "3"), DERIVED (not looked up) by
% bridging a biology fact to a math fact (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY,
% cross-directory composition with MATHEMATICS). Grounds the common
% classroom framing "anaphase is the THIRD phase of mitosis."
% ============================================================================
% `biology/mitosis-phase-order.adj` already tables each phase's position as a
% NUMBER (anaphase is position 3); `mathematics/ordinal-numbers.adj` already
% tables each NUMBER's ordinal WORD (3 is "third"). Neither file states
% outright "anaphase is the third phase" — but composing the two
% ALREADY-SHIPPED facts answers it directly. This is the SAME "compose two
% independently-citable facts into a derived, dual-cited conclusion"
% discipline `astronomy/planet-ordinal-position.adj` and
% `astronomy/moon-phase-ordinal-position.adj` already established — the
% FOURTH cross-directory `rule` composition in this loop's science sweep,
% and the FIRST in the biology domain.
%
%     import "mitosis-phase-ordinal-position.adj"
%     ? mitosis_phase_ordinal_position(anaphase, $O)     % third
%     ? mitosis_phase_ordinal_position($P, first)        % prophase (reverse recall)
%     ? mitosis_phase_ordinal_position(interphase, $O)   % abstain -- not one of the 4 ordered mitotic phases
%
% This is a native `rule` (ADJ-TABLES RS-6): its body binds `$Phase`/`$Number`
% through the already-shipped `mitosis_phase_order` table, then re-binds
% `$Number`/`$Ordinal` through the already-shipped `ordinal_number` table — a
% plain two-hop join (the same shape the astronomy ordinal bridges already
% established), not a causal inference; it just names the standard convention
% that a counting number has a corresponding ordinal word. A query's `steps`
% trail shows THREE entries: the rule's own citation (the ordinal-word
% convention), the `mitosis_phase_order` fact's citation (NCI SEER), and the
% `ordinal_number` fact's citation, so a human can independently verify each
% link. Honest abstention on `interphase` — the resting phase BETWEEN
% divisions, deliberately NOT a row in `mitosis-phase-order.adj` (mirroring
% `mitosis-phases.adj`'s own exclusion) — this rule abstains on it too,
% rather than inventing a position.
%
% CROSS-DIRECTORY IMPORT: this file lives in `biology/` (its natural subject
% home, alongside `mitosis-phase-order.adj`) and imports its mathematics
% sibling with a relative `../mathematics/ordinal-numbers.adj` path — the
% SAME shape `earth-science/season-start-month-number.adj` and
% `astronomy/planet-ordinal-position.adj` already established and
% empirically verified (in a scratch dir, before writing this file, following
% that precedent exactly): the CLI's import sandbox is rooted at the
% TOP-LEVEL PROGRAM's own directory, not each importer's directory, so this
% resolves cleanly provided the consuming entry program lives at or above the
% package root. This library's OWN `.query.adj` companion is therefore placed
% at the package root (not beside this file), mirroring
% `planet-ordinal-position.query.adj` and
% `moon-phase-ordinal-position.query.adj`'s already-established pattern.
%
% Deliberately scoped to the STANDARD ordinal-word bridge only, over the
% canonical four mitotic phases `mitosis-phase-order.adj` already tables — no
% new phase or ordinal-number content is asserted here, only the join. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

import "mitosis-phase-order.adj"
import "../mathematics/ordinal-numbers.adj"

rule { head: mitosis_phase_ordinal_position($Phase, $Ordinal)
       when: mitosis_phase_order($Phase, $Number), ordinal_number($Number, $Ordinal)
       source "1 one first, 2 two second, 3 three third, 4 four fourth, 5 five fifth"
       locator "https://www.ef.edu/english-resources/english-grammar/numbers-english/"
       trust consensus }
